ABSTRACT
A0 specifies that an agentic ST-T1024 decomposes goals into sub-tasks via a ROM-burned
plan library. A0 does not specify what a plan is. This document specifies it.
Without A3, DECOMPOSE has no target. The plan library has no schema. The bounded-
plan assumption of the Verifiable Agency Theorem (|p| ≤ K) has no grounding. A3 is the
second load-bearing premise of the agentic architecture, complementing A4.
This document defines:
1. The plan schema — what a plan is, structurally.
2. The plan library — how plans are stored, indexed, and versioned.
3. The composition rules — how plans compose without unbounded recursion.
4. The bounded length property — why every plan has a finite maximum length.
5. The DECOMPOSE semantics — how a plan becomes a sub-task sequence.
6. The failure and rollback semantics — what happens when a sub-task fails.
Every definition is anchored to a requirement of the Verifiable Agency Theorem. Every
constraint is a design decision.
Status: Specification. No reference implementation. No hardware validation. 1. INTRODUCTION: WHY PLANS COME SECOND
1.1 The Dependency Chain
```
State (A4) + Action Library (A2)
↓
Plans (A3)
├── DECOMPOSE (A1) — plans become sub-task sequences
├── Composition (A8) — plans compose via declared rules
├── Bounded length — supports the Verifiable Agency Theorem
└── Log entries (A7) — plan execution is logged
```
A4 gives the agent a state. A2 gives the agent actions. A3 gives the agent sequences of
actions — plans — that achieve goals. Without A3, the agent is a collection of isolated
actions with no way to combine them into meaningful tasks.
1.2 The Two Constraints
The Verifiable Agency Theorem requires:
· Finiteness — |P| < ∞ (bounded plan library).
· Bounded length — ∀p ∈ P: |p| ≤ K (bounded plan length).
A3 must deliver both. Not by hoping plans are short, but by constructing a representation
that cannot exceed K.
1.3 The Design Principle
A plan is a finite, deterministic, acyclic sequence of sub-tasks — where each sub-task is
either an action from A2, a nested plan from P, or a bounded conditional on state from A4.
Plans do not invent. They compose what already exists. The plan library is the agent's
procedural ROM.
