5. COMPOSITION RULES
5.1 The Three Composition Operators
Plans compose via three declared operators, all bounded:
Sequential composition (;). p1 ; p2 — execute p1, then p2. Both plans must be in P. Length
adds: |p1 ; p2| = |p1| + |p2|. Bound: sum ≤ K.
Conditional composition (if). if predicate then p1 else p2. Both branches must be in P.
Length: max(|p1|, |p2|) + 1. Bound: max + 1 ≤ K.
Bounded iteration (while_bounded). while_bounded predicate max_n p. Iteration count is
bounded by declared max_n. Length: max_n × |p| + 1. Bound: product ≤ K.
Rule: composition is compile-time. Plans are composed when burned into ROM, not at
runtime. Runtime composition is forbidden, because it would violate bounded length.
5.2 No Unbounded Recursion
Rule: the PlanStep invocation graph must be a DAG. Recursive plans are rejected at ROM
burn time.
Proof of feasibility. DAG property is checkable at compile time by topological sort. Plans
failing the check are not admitted to P. □
5.3 Total Bounded Length
Proposition. Every composed plan has |p| ≤ K.
Proof. By induction over the composition operators. Base: atomic actions have length 1 ≤ K.
Sequential: sum of bounded terms, bounded by K by declared constraint. Conditional: max
of bounded terms plus 1, bounded by K. Iteration: product of bounded term and max_n,
bounded by K. DAG property prevents infinite expansion. □ 6. DECOMPOSE SEMANTICS
6.1 Definition
Definition 5 (DECOMPOSE). Given a selected plan p, DECOMPOSE produces a sub-task
sequence:
```
DECOMPOSE(p, s) : (Plan, State) → Sequence(SubTask)
```
The sequence is finite, ordered, and deterministic.
6.2 The Decomposition Rules
Plan Node Decomposition
ActionStep(a, params) One sub-task: invoke action a with params
PlanStep(p', params) Recursive DECOMPOSE, up to D_invoke depth
ConditionalStep(pred, t, e) Evaluate pred on s; decompose selected branch
The sequence is built depth-first, in declared order.
6.3 Sub-Task Ordering
Sub-tasks execute in sequence. Each sub-task:
1. Reads its declared state projection (A4 §5.1).
2. Executes (via A1 FSM).
3. Verifies (via A5).
4. Commits or refuses.
5. Logs (via A7).
If a sub-task refuses: see §7.
6.4 Bounded Decomposition
Proposition. |DECOMPOSE(p, s)| ≤ K × D_invoke.
Proof. Each ActionStep produces one sub-task. Each PlanStep recursively expands,
bounded by D_invoke. Total sub-tasks bounded by plan length × invocation depth. □
