7. FAILURE AND ROLLBACK SEMANTICS
7.1 Failure Modes Failure Cause Response
Sub-task refused VERIFY = FAIL Apply plan's declared failure policy
Sub-task timeout T-AET exceeded 0x-BUDGET-EXCEEDED
Invariant violated State left I 0x-UNSAFE-ACT, halt
Plan preconditions unmet State drifted 0x-AMBIG-GOAL, refuse plan
7.2 Failure Policy
Each plan declares a failure policy in its schema:
```
FailurePolicy ::=
| Halt \* Stop, log, refuse
| Retry(n) \* Retry sub-task up to n times
| RollbackTo(step) \* Return to step k, retry from there
| Skip \* Continue to next sub-task (dangerous, discouraged)
```
Rule: default policy is Halt. Deployments may override, but every override is declared and
auditable.
Rule: retry count is bounded. The total cycles consumed by a plan — including retries —
must not exceed budget_max.
7.3 Rollback
Rollback restores state to a prior checkpoint. Checkpoints are recorded in the append-only
log (A7). Rollback itself is an action with its own envelope; it must pass VERIFY like any
other action.
Rule: rollback cannot violate invariants. If the state before the failure was in I, and rollback
returns to that state, I is preserved.
